import{_ as t,o as a,c as n,a2 as o}from"./chunks/framework.CDU0sSpB.js";const u=JSON.parse('{"title":"Agent Reference Patterns: Pi, OpenClaw, Hermes, DeerFlow, OpenCode, and LoopX","description":"Patterns Hearth adopts or rejects for a general execution kernel, proactive service, desktop operation, memory, method evolution, multi-device use, and long-running operation.","frontmatter":{"title":"Agent Reference Patterns: Pi, OpenClaw, Hermes, DeerFlow, OpenCode, and LoopX","description":"Patterns Hearth adopts or rejects for a general execution kernel, proactive service, desktop operation, memory, method evolution, multi-device use, and long-running operation.","outline":[2,3]},"headers":[],"relativePath":"en/design/agent-reference-patterns.md","filePath":"en/design/agent-reference-patterns.md","lastUpdated":1794373393000}'),r={name:"en/design/agent-reference-patterns.md"};function i(s,e,d,l,c,p){return a(),n("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[o('<h1 id="agent-reference-patterns-pi-openclaw-hermes-deerflow-opencode-and-loopx" tabindex="-1">Agent Reference Patterns: Pi, OpenClaw, Hermes, DeerFlow, OpenCode, and LoopX <a class="header-anchor" href="#agent-reference-patterns-pi-openclaw-hermes-deerflow-opencode-and-loopx" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Agent Reference Patterns: Pi, OpenClaw, Hermes, DeerFlow, OpenCode, and LoopX&quot;">​</a></h1><p>Across Phases 3–6, Hearth does not invent a general execution kernel, device control, proactive service, memory, method evaluation, or long-running operation from scratch. We studied working open agents and adopted only the parts that reduce accidental input, unauthorized expansion, wrong memory, and irreversible change. These notes reflect public implementation or design documentation and a 2026-11-11 review of Hearth&#39;s current code.</p><p>This page records design references, not integration claims. Hearth does not import these projects&#39; runtimes, codebases, or protocol implementations. Pi influences the message loop and tool shape of the native agent kernel; DeerFlow influences long-horizon orchestration boundaries; OpenCode influences the capability choices of the general execution surface; LoopX influences task decomposition, evaluation layering, and delivery method. OpenCode&#39;s installable npm version and public documentation have not always described the same behavior, so Hearth learns only from the public capability shape and neither copies its runtime nor pins behavior to a version.</p><h2 id="shared-conclusions" tabindex="-1">Shared conclusions <a class="header-anchor" href="#shared-conclusions" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Shared conclusions&quot;">​</a></h2><p>OpenClaw and Hermes separate connection, capability grants, action execution, and human takeover. A connected device is not an authorized device. A one-time approval is not a permanent grant. The model may reference the current observation only; it cannot hold long-lived credentials or silently fall back outside the approved capability and window scope. Phases 4–6 apply the same rule: a suggestion is not an execution, a candidate is not an adopted capability, and a backup file is not proof of a complete migration.</p><h2 id="openclaw-patterns-worth-adopting" tabindex="-1">OpenClaw patterns worth adopting <a class="header-anchor" href="#openclaw-patterns-worth-adopting" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;OpenClaw patterns worth adopting&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li><strong>Pairing is separate from capability approval.</strong> Pairing only establishes the connection. Declared commands or capabilities require another approval. Execution must satisfy declared, approved, and gateway-policy checks together.</li><li><strong>Deny rules win.</strong> Extending capabilities requires a new approval, and revoking a device immediately invalidates its grants.</li><li><strong>Short-lived pairing, durable capability records.</strong> Pairing requests expire quickly. No capability is available before approval.</li><li><strong>Observation is distinct from action.</strong> Screen snapshots carry a frame ID, dimensions, and execution ID. Coordinate references expire. Desktop actions use <code>computer.act</code>; observation uses <code>screen.snapshot</code>.</li><li><strong>Takeover precedes resumption.</strong> Human takeover pauses agent input and cancels pending input. The agent must explicitly take control again and observe anew before resuming.</li><li><strong>Fail closed.</strong> If observation fails, the control provider changes, or a request is out of scope, return a structured refusal rather than silently choosing another path.</li><li><strong>Proactive suggestions are separate from execution.</strong> Proactive mode first produces an explainable suggestion. Accepting it or converting it to a task still follows the normal task and approval chain; being “proactive” grants no extra authority.</li></ul><h2 id="hermes-patterns-worth-adopting" tabindex="-1">Hermes patterns worth adopting <a class="header-anchor" href="#hermes-patterns-worth-adopting" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Hermes patterns worth adopting&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li><strong>Explicit permission modes.</strong> <code>standard</code>, <code>bounded</code>, and <code>unrestricted</code> are fixed at startup. <code>bounded</code> requires one approval of a capability manifest and fails closed on out-of-scope actions.</li><li><strong>Native Windows input.</strong> UIAutomation plus <code>SendInput</code>/<code>PostMessage</code> drive actions while avoiding unnecessary foreground-focus changes.</li><li><strong>Short-lived references.</strong> Element references belong to the current screenshot or observation generation and fail clearly when stale.</li><li><strong>Independent health checks.</strong> A <code>doctor</code> path exposes missing drivers, insufficient permissions, and Session 0 problems before a task half-executes.</li><li><strong>Credentials remain in the main process.</strong> Device connection credentials are not exposed to the renderer or ordinary plugins.</li><li><strong>Cancellation and no silent downgrade.</strong> Cancellation is explicit, and the capability manifest cannot change silently during a run.</li><li><strong>Diagnosable runtime state.</strong> Health checks should run independently of tasks and expose the runtime environment, permissions, and latest maintenance state early. Hearth extends this into health snapshots, maintenance records, and redacted diagnostics exports.</li></ul><h2 id="pi-patterns-worth-adopting" tabindex="-1">Pi patterns worth adopting <a class="header-anchor" href="#pi-patterns-worth-adopting" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Pi patterns worth adopting&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li><strong>A small, explicit single-agent loop.</strong> Model turn, tool call, tool result, next turn. Hearth does not start with complex multi-agent orchestration.</li><li><strong>Messages are state.</strong> System prompts, user input, assistant tool calls, and tool results are stored in order. Recovery continues the same sequence instead of rebuilding a prompt.</li><li><strong>Tools are capability boundaries.</strong> Reading, writing, shell, network, and user questions have separate task permissions. The model cannot expand them mid-run.</li><li><strong>Recoverable execution.</strong> Checkpoints are written after turns and tool steps. Waiting input, budget exhaustion, and service restart resume from an explicit position.</li><li><strong>A compact completion signal.</strong> A completion tool declares the result, and the system verifies artifacts. The model saying “done” is not completion.</li></ul><p>Hearth implements these principles in its own <code>@hearth/agent-runtime</code> without importing the Pi npm package. The native kernel keeps a small, explicit loop while adding token-aware compaction, transient retries, loop detection, on-demand skills, bounded delegation, local MCP, and worktrees. These are Hearth task-control capabilities, not a claim of feature parity with Pi. Codex remains the default executor for GitHub engineering work, and both paths share task state, permissions, budgets, and the artifact contract.</p><h2 id="deerflow-patterns-worth-adopting" tabindex="-1">DeerFlow patterns worth adopting <a class="header-anchor" href="#deerflow-patterns-worth-adopting" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;DeerFlow patterns worth adopting&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li><strong>Task decomposition and staged evidence.</strong> Break a complex task into inspectable stages, then let later steps reference prior evidence instead of handing the entire goal to one opaque execution.</li><li><strong>Separate research, generation, and evaluation.</strong> Candidate production and candidate evaluation can be separate, so one run does not both change a method and grade its own change.</li><li><strong>Middleware and lazy tools.</strong> Long tasks need separate handling for context, tool availability, memory, and subtasks instead of putting every capability into one prompt.</li><li><strong>Sandbox, subagent, and memory boundaries.</strong> Subtasks need independent boundaries, and long-lived information needs provenance and a recall budget.</li></ul><p>Hearth borrows this decomposition, evidence, layered-evaluation, and long-horizon middleware structure for method-candidate generation, baseline/trial comparison, and native compaction, recovery, and bounded delegation. It does not integrate DeerFlow or adopt its multi-agent collaboration runtime. DeerFlow still has broader coverage of long-horizon orchestration, sandboxes, subagents, skills, memory, and lazy tools than the current native kernel.</p><h2 id="opencode-patterns-worth-adopting" tabindex="-1">OpenCode patterns worth adopting <a class="header-anchor" href="#opencode-patterns-worth-adopting" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;OpenCode patterns worth adopting&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li><strong>Execution surface before orchestration.</strong> glob/grep, atomic editing, shell, skills, subagents, MCP, compaction, and worktrees together form general task completion, not just one read/write loop.</li><li><strong>Edits must be previewable.</strong> Show a diff before modifying, and validate every patch target before one atomic write so a multi-file patch cannot partially succeed.</li><li><strong>Tools are discoverable capabilities.</strong> Skills and extension tools load on demand; MCP server management, tool discovery, and call lifecycle are modeled separately.</li><li><strong>Delegation has explicit boundaries.</strong> A subagent starts with fresh context, writes in an isolated worktree, and leaves the parent to inspect and merge the result.</li><li><strong>Context must be actively managed.</strong> Compact at the model window, retain recent context and complete tool pairs, and make compaction part of recovery.</li></ul><p>Hearth has adopted these public capability shapes with its own glob/grep, atomic edits, bounded shell output and process-tree timeout, office artifacts, search, skill index, bounded subagents, local stdio MCP, and project worktrees. It has not copied OpenCode&#39;s code or runtime; approval, the task ledger, checkpoints, recovery, and artifact verification remain Hearth implementations. The remaining gap is mainly LSP, formatters, remote MCP, browser automation, visual understanding, automatic merging, and a more complete worktree experience.</p><h2 id="loopx-patterns-worth-adopting" tabindex="-1">LoopX patterns worth adopting <a class="header-anchor" href="#loopx-patterns-worth-adopting" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;LoopX patterns worth adopting&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li><strong>Small closed-loop delivery.</strong> Converge one verifiable capability change at a time instead of packaging several unverified features into one “big completion”.</li><li><strong>State and evidence together.</strong> A delivery note records verified paths, explicit gaps, and claims that are not allowed. A page or passing test does not replace real acceptance.</li><li><strong>Acceptance by boundary.</strong> High-risk capabilities such as devices, backup, and long-running operation require their own failure-path drills, not only a happy-path demonstration.</li></ul><p>Hearth uses LoopX only as a delivery-method reference. It is not a runtime dependency, and its workflow mechanics are not embedded in the Hearth scheduler.</p><h2 id="hearth-adoption-boundary" tabindex="-1">Hearth adoption boundary <a class="header-anchor" href="#hearth-adoption-boundary" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Hearth adoption boundary&quot;">​</a></h2><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Decision</th><th>Hearth behavior</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Device identity</td><td>A same-machine Windows helper creates a short-lived pairing request over <code>127.0.0.1</code>. The user enters its pairing code in Hearth. The device token remains in the helper and server only.</td></tr><tr><td>Capability approval</td><td>Successful pairing grants nothing. Observation, desktop control, and UI automation are approved separately and bound to an application/window allowlist. Revocation is immediate.</td></tr><tr><td>Observation reference</td><td>Every observation returns a snapshot with frame ID, dimensions, and timestamp. Actions carry the frame ID; the server rechecks device presence, capability, controller, and observation generation before dispatch.</td></tr><tr><td>Human takeover</td><td>Pause, resume, takeover, release, and stop are first-class states. Takeover stops dispatch and asks the helper to cancel pending input. Release requires a new observation.</td></tr><tr><td>Action granularity</td><td>Only discrete low-risk actions inside allowlisted applications, such as click, short text entry, and key presses. Every action returns before/after observation evidence and a structured receipt.</td></tr><tr><td>Data retention</td><td>Screenshots stay in the local device data directory with short expiry. The server records audit events, not the full screen, in the event log.</td></tr><tr><td>Failure handling</td><td>Missing drivers, insufficient permissions, lost windows, scope mismatch, or unverifiable results stop the flow and return control to the user. High-risk actions are not retried, and success is never fabricated.</td></tr><tr><td>Proactive service</td><td>Produce only suggestions with source, why-now, suggested action, impact, and risk. Accept records a decision; convert creates a queued task; external actions still require approval. Daily limits, quiet hours, cooldown, and pause constrain interruption.</td></tr><tr><td>Tool-level approval</td><td>The native executor asks for one-time approval before writing or editing files, applying patches, running shell commands, fetching web pages, capturing the desktop, and calling MCP tools. Approval covers only that call; MCP approval binds the server, tool, and argument digest. Rejection returns a tool error to the model and the task continues. DeepSeek Harness <code>approval/request</code> calls reuse the same one-time approval record and map to <code>allowed-once</code>, <code>rejected</code>, or <code>cancelled</code>. Full permission auto-allows and records an audit event. Codex, Hermes, Qoder, WorkBuddy, and Kimi native per-tool approval events are not yet normalized, and external effects remain governed by the separate effect ledger.</td></tr><tr><td>Memory</td><td>Memory starts as a candidate and needs user confirmation. Recall records reasons and budgets; injected context is marked untrusted background. Sensitive memory is not recalled by default, <code>secret</code> is never recalled, and forgetting redacts projections, versions, events, and recall audits.</td></tr><tr><td>Method evolution</td><td>A candidate can only inherit the intersection of source-task capabilities. Trials read existing terminal traces and create no external side effects. Only repeatable improvement evidence permits promotion, with rejection, rollback, and reasons retained.</td></tr><tr><td>Multi-device execution</td><td>Task leases use owner, TTL, and epoch. Expired takeover advances the epoch and rejects the old executor&#39;s commits. Sync copies only filtered event projections, not approvals, sources, outbox, device, or desktop-control events.</td></tr><tr><td>Revocation and migration</td><td>Device capabilities, device tokens, and sync peers can be revoked or rotated. The global kill switch blocks approvals and scheduler restart. Old-device invalidation needs a real drill, and database restore is described separately from artifact migration.</td></tr><tr><td>Backup and restore</td><td>Use a passphrase-encrypted database archive. Validate format, schema, integrity, and columns against a temporary database before transactional replacement, roll back on failure, and explicitly exclude artifact bodies, workspaces, attachments, and screenshots.</td></tr><tr><td>Long-running operation</td><td>Health, maintenance, and diagnostics are first-class. Maintenance cleans expired operational state without deleting tasks, memories, or artifacts. A failed maintenance run degrades health and never relaxes approval as “automatic repair”.</td></tr></tbody></table><h2 id="out-of-scope-for-this-phase" tabindex="-1">Out of scope for this phase <a class="header-anchor" href="#out-of-scope-for-this-phase" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Out of scope for this phase&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li>Public remote device access, shared multi-user devices, or cross-device screen streaming.</li><li>Global keyboard and mouse control outside the approved window allowlist.</li><li>Password managers, system security settings, payments, and irreversible file operations.</li><li>Sending screenshots automatically to chat channels or exposing device tokens to the renderer.</li><li>Pretending a real Windows helper was verified. Anything not testable in the current environment remains disabled and is listed in delivery status.</li><li>Turning proactive suggestions into an auto-executor or skipping approval because the system originated the suggestion.</li><li>Treating candidate memory as fact or a candidate method as an adopted capability.</li><li>Claiming system push, phone pairing, remote takeover, or real mobile-device acceptance. The PWA currently provides an installable shell and offline navigation only.</li><li>Describing DeerFlow, OpenCode, or LoopX as integrated components. They provide design, capability-tradeoff, and delivery-method references only.</li><li>Describing Pi as an integrated dependency. Hearth uses its single-agent kernel shape as a reference and provides its own TypeScript implementation.</li><li>Treating OpenCode&#39;s public execution surface as something Hearth has fully copied. LSP, formatters, remote MCP, browser automation, visual understanding, and automatic merging are not implemented.</li></ul><h2 id="impact-by-phase" tabindex="-1">Impact by phase <a class="header-anchor" href="#impact-by-phase" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Impact by phase&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li><strong>Phase 2:</strong> Drafts and approvals are separate, approvals are short-lived and single-use, approved effects write only to the local outbox rather than pretending to send mail, duplicate confirmations do not duplicate writes, and failures distinguish “not written” from “outcome uncertain”.</li><li><strong>Phase 3:</strong> Device pairing, capability approval, observation frames, human takeover, and control epochs are modeled separately. Actions that cannot be observed or verified are refused rather than silently downgraded.</li><li><strong>Phase 4:</strong> Proactive suggestions provide decision material only. Accept, ignore, snooze, and convert are first-class actions. Attention budgets, quiet hours, and pause take precedence over “more reminders”.</li><li><strong>Phase 5:</strong> Memory and methods start as candidates; recall and method use retain source and reason. Forgetting, rejection, and rollback are verifiable first-class paths.</li><li><strong>Phase 6:</strong> Sync, leases, revocation, backup restore, health, and maintenance default to fail-closed or rollback. A happy path does not replace lost-device, offline, or multi-week drills.</li></ul>',27)])])}const g=t(r,[["render",i]]);export{u as __pageData,g as default};
